---
// WHAT THIS COMPONENT IS:
// The MLD Marketing Solutions wordmark rendered as an inline SVG with a glass-tint finish.
// The path data was image-traced from the original flat SVG logo in Downloads/MLD Logo.svg.
//
// WHY INLINE SVG AND NOT AN <img>:
// I need to apply gradient fills and a reflection layer directly to the path.
// An <img> tag is a black box - I can't touch its internals with CSS.
//
// THE PROP:
// className controls size only. Pass Tailwind height/width utilities.
// Example: className="h-8 w-auto" (navbar), className="h-10 w-auto" (footer).
// The SVG scales via preserveAspectRatio so width:auto always works from a height alone.
// The viewBox is 1095x428 so the natural aspect ratio is ~2.56:1 (wide wordmark).

interface Props {
  className?: string;
}
const { className = 'h-8 w-auto' } = Astro.props;
---

<!--
  WHY NO TEXT-COLOR UTILITIES HERE:
  The fills are explicit SVG gradients (url(#mld-tint), url(#mld-reflect)).
  currentColor has no effect on gradient fills. If I pass text-foreground or
  text-accent from a parent, nothing happens. Don't add those - they're dead weight.

  THE OUTER CSS CLASS (.glass-svg-logo) LIVES IN global.css:
  It is a filter: drop-shadow() chain. That's the only shadow type that clips to
  the actual path shape. CSS box-shadow and text-shadow don't follow SVG contours.
  Three shadows stacked:
    1. 0 1px 0  white 0.28  = top edge highlight (simulates overhead light hitting the rim)
    2. 0 -1px 0 black 0.18  = bottom edge micro-shadow (grounds the shape slightly)
    3. 0 6px 18px black 0.5 = depth shadow below (lifts the logo off the background)
  Hover intensifies all three values - see global.css .glass-svg-logo:hover.

  WHY NOT backdrop-filter ON THE SVG:
  backdrop-filter would blur whatever is behind the element's bounding box rectangle,
  not the path shape. So I'd get a blurred rectangle, not a blurred logo outline.
  The glass look here is entirely achieved through fill opacity + reflection layer instead.
-->

<svg
  viewBox="0 0 1095 428"
  class={`glass-svg-logo ${className}`}
  style="fill-rule:evenodd;clip-rule:evenodd;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-miterlimit:2;"
  preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"
  aria-hidden="true"
>
  <!--
    fill-rule:evenodd is CRITICAL for this path.
    The logo has interlocking subpaths (the brain/circuit section especially).
    evenodd determines which overlapping regions are filled vs punched out.
    Change this to nonzero and parts of the brain graphic will fill incorrectly.
  -->

  <defs>

    <!--
      GRADIENT 1: BRAND TINT (mld-tint)
      This is the main glass colour. Yellow on the left, teal on the right.
      It sits at partial opacity so the dark background bleeds through - that's
      what makes it read as translucent/glass rather than opaque coloured ink.

      TUNING GUIDE:
      - stop-opacity on both stops = how vivid the colour is.
        0.0 = invisible, 1.0 = fully opaque, no glass feel.
        0.5-0.75 is the useful range. Below 0.4 the colour gets too washed out.
      - The yellow stop is slightly lower opacity (0.62) than the teal (0.92)
        because yellow is a lighter hue and reads stronger at the same opacity.
        Balancing them visually is more important than matching the numbers.
      - x1/x2 at 0%/100% means the gradient runs full width of the path bounding box.
        If the logo ever changes shape, the gradient remaps automatically.
      - gradientUnits defaults to objectBoundingBox here (no explicit attribute),
        so 0% and 100% are relative to the path's own bounding box, not the viewBox.
    -->
    <linearGradient id="mld-tint" x1="0%" y1="0%" x2="100%" y2="0%">
      <stop offset="0%"   stop-color="#F69422" stop-opacity="0.62"/>
      <stop offset="50%"   stop-color="#F69422" stop-opacity="0.62"/>
      <stop offset="100%" stop-color="#0CA99E" stop-opacity="0.92"/>
    </linearGradient>

    <!--
      GRADIENT 2: GLASS REFLECTION (mld-reflect)
      A diagonal white gradient that simulates light hitting the top-left face of
      a glass object. This is what separates "coloured fill" from "glass object".
      It runs at high opacity near the origin and fades out roughly halfway across.

      TUNING GUIDE:
      - First stop (0%) = the bright hot-spot. 0.98 is almost white. Lower it if
        the reflection looks like a paint stroke rather than a light catch.
        0.3-0.5 is subtle, 0.7-1.0 is dramatic.
      - x2="85%" y2="65%" controls the direction and reach of the fade.
        x2 and y2 are both percentages of the bounding box.
        Pushing x2 closer to 100% makes the reflection cover more of the width.
        A steeper angle (x2 low, y2 high) shifts it toward vertical.
      - The third stop at 0.05 (not 0.0) means the reflection never fully vanishes
        at the bottom-right, giving a faint ambient shimmer across the whole shape.
        Set it to 0.0 for a harder falloff.
    -->
    <linearGradient id="mld-reflect" x1="0%" y1="0%" x2="75%" y2="75%">
      <stop offset="0%"   stop-color="#ffffff" stop-opacity="0.78"/>
      <stop offset="45%"  stop-color="#ffffff" stop-opacity="0.08"/>
      <stop offset="100%" stop-color="#ffffff" stop-opacity="0.05"/>
    </linearGradient>

    <!--
      THE PATH (mld-path)
      Defined once here in <defs>, referenced twice below with <use>.
      This avoids duplicating 3KB of path data in the markup.
      The path is the full compound shape: ML letterforms + the brain/circuit icon.
      It came from image-tracing the flat logo in Affinity/Inkscape.

      DO NOT EDIT THE PATH D ATTRIBUTE by hand - it will break the shape.
      If the logo is retraced, paste the new d value here only, nowhere else.
      The <use> references below pick it up automatically.
