  <!--
    THE TRANSFORM CHAIN:
    These three nested <g> transforms come directly from the original Affinity/Inkscape export.
    The tracer outputs coordinates relative to a much larger artboard, then applies transforms
    to position them into the 0 0 1095 428 viewBox space.
    I don't touch these. If I flatten them and re-export the path in viewBox-native coordinates,
    I can remove the three <g> wrappers - but that requires re-tracing or a transform-flatten
    step in Affinity. Until then, leave them exactly as they are.

    Matrix 1: translate(-479.97, -1422.46) - shifts the artboard origin
    Matrix 2: scale(1.172) + translate(-146, 1002) - scales and repositions
    Matrix 3: scale(1.027) + translate(467, 319) - fine-tunes final placement
  -->
  <g transform="matrix(1,0,0,1,-479.97268,-1422.458155)">
    <g transform="matrix(1.172394,0,0,1.172394,-146.053932,1002.435902)">
      <g transform="matrix(1.027344,0,0,1.027344,467,319)">

        <!--
          LAYER 1: BRAND TINT
          The main glass colour. Semi-transparent gradient fill so the dark background
          shows through. This is what makes it look like glass rather than painted ink.
          Adjust stop-opacity values in <defs> above to change how vivid or how glassy.
        -->
        <use href="#mld-path" fill="url(#mld-tint)"/>

        <!--
          LAYER 2: REFLECTION
          Stacked on top of the tint. A white diagonal gradient at decreasing opacity.
          It catches the upper-left corner bright and fades out toward the bottom-right.
          This is the visual cue that makes a flat surface read as a 3D glass object.
          Adjust the first stop-opacity in <defs> to control how strong the shine is.
          Too high (>0.9) and it looks like a paint smear. Too low (<0.2) and it disappears.
        -->
        <use href="#mld-path" fill="url(#mld-reflect)"/>

      </g>
    </g>
  </g>
</svg>
